Here is a more detailed and thorough answer to your previous question:

To generate a document from a given base URL using Java, you can use the Jsoup library.

**Generating the Document**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String baseUri = "https://example.com";
        String htmlContent = generateDocument(baseUri);
        System.out.println(htmlContent);
    }

    public static String generateDocument(String baseUri) {
        try (Document document = Jsoup.connect(baseUri).get()) {
            return document.outerHtml();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `Jsoup.connect` method to establish a connection to the base URL. We then use the `get()` method to retrieve the HTML content of the page.

**Getting Forms**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String baseUri = "https://example.com";
        List<FormElement> forms = getForms(baseUri);
        for (FormElement form : forms) {
            System.out.println(form.getName() + ": " + form.getText());
        }
    }

    public static List<FormElement> getForms(String baseUri) {
        Document document = generateDocument(baseUri);
        return document.select("form");
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `select()` method to retrieve all forms on the page. We then iterate over each form and print its name and text.

**Getting Titles**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String baseUri = "https://example.com";
        List<String> titles = getTitles(baseUri);
        for (String title : titles) {
            System.out.println(title);
        }
    }

    public static List<String> getTitles(String baseUri) {
        Document document = generateDocument(baseUri);
        return document.select("title").map(String::trim).toList();
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `select()` method to retrieve all title elements on the page. We then iterate over each title and print it.

**Form Element Class**

```java
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;

public class FormElement {
    private String name;
    private String text;

    public FormElement(String name, String text) {
        this.name = name;
        this.text = text;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
        FormElement that = (FormElement) o;
        return Objects.equals(name, that.name) && Objects.equals(text, that.text);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(name, text);
    }
}
```

In this example, we're defining a `FormElement` class with two properties: `name` and `text`. We've also added getters and an overridden `equals()` method to support comparison of form elements.

