**Generating the Document**

To generate a document from a given base URL using Java, you can use the Jsoup library.

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String baseUri = "https://example.com";
        String htmlContent = generateDocument(baseUri);
        System.out.println(htmlContent);
    }

    public static String generateDocument(String baseUri) {
        try (Document document = Jsoup.connect(baseUri).get()) {
            return document.outerHtml();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```

This code connects to the base URL, gets the HTML content of the page, and returns it as a string.

**Getting Forms**

To get all forms on a webpage using Jsoup, you can use the following method:

```java
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String baseUri = "https://example.com";
        List<FormElement> forms = getForms(baseUri);
        for (FormElement form : forms) {
            System.out.println(form.getName() + ": " + form.getText());
        }
    }

    public static List<FormElement> getForms(String baseUri) {
        Document document = generateDocument(baseUri);
        return document.select("form");
    }
}
```

This code connects to the base URL, gets the HTML content of the page, and selects all forms on the page using the `select()` method. The result is a list of form elements.

**Getting Titles**

To get all titles (h1-h6 headings) on a webpage using Jsoup, you can use the following code:

```java
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

